We have that $z^{997}=1$ since all of the roots of $z^{1997}-1=0$ are the 35th roots of 1.  If $z = e^{2\pi i k/1997}$ is a 35th root of 1, then $|z| = 1.$  We can complete the factorization $(z-e^{2\pi i k/35})(z-e^{2\pi i (k+1)/35})\cdots(z-e^{2\pi i (k+9)/35}) = z^{1997}-1=0,$ and then taking the absolute value, we get $|e^{2\pi i k/35}|=|e^{2\pi i (k+1)/35}|\cdots|e^{2\pi i (k+9)/35}|=1.$

Our problem becomes the probability that $\sqrt{2+\sqrt{3}} \le |e^{i\theta}-e^{i\phi}|.$  Note that $|e^{i\theta}-e^{i\phi}| = \sqrt{2-\left(e^{i\theta} + e^{i\phi}\right)+2\cos(\theta-\phi)} = \sqrt{2+\sqrt{3}}$ if and only if $\cos(\theta-\phi) \ge -\frac{1}{2}.$

We know that if $z = \cos(\theta) + i\sin(\theta),$ then $\arg(z) = \theta.$  Thus, $\cos\left(\theta-\frac{-\pi}{6}\right) \ge -\frac{1}{2}.$  If we let $f(x) = \cos(x),$ then this means that the probability of this event happening is $\frac12.$

We want the probability that $v$ and $w$ are in the same conjugate root group, which means the probability that $v$ and $w$ are complex conjugates.  Multiplying by 2, we get $\frac12$ of the 1996 total cases, or 997.

Note: It turns out that complex conjugate roots of unity are also the only roots of unity that satisfy this inequality.  It is worth noting that there are even more answers than shown here!

